<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 16</p>
  <h1>Money & spending: <em>follow the amount, direction, and purpose</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Money language becomes precise when you ask five questions: <span class="ex">What amount is named?</span> <span class="ex">Is money coming in or going out?</span> <span class="ex">Who gives it and who receives it?</span> <span class="ex">Is the speaker describing price, total cost, affordability, or value?</span> <span class="ex">Which charges and time conditions belong to the decision?</span> The same purchase can be affordable but poor value, low-priced but unsuitable, or expensive now but less costly over time.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">income · expenses · countability</span>
    <span class="tag">cost · charge · pay · borrow · lend · owe</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Separate a listed price, added charge, total cost, available amount, and value judgment.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Distinguish</b><span>Track income and expenses, countability, transaction roles, and U.S. checkout language.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Use accurate patterns for paying, spending, charging, borrowing, lending, owing, and saving.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Decide</b><span>Explain a fictional purchase from stated priorities without giving financial advice or disclosing private information.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can distinguish salary, wages, income, revenue, expenses, and savings; use countable and uncountable money nouns accurately; track the roles in cost, charge, pay, spend, borrow, lend, owe, and pay back; separate listed price, additional charges, total cost, affordability, and value; understand common U.S. words such as check, bill, receipt, fee, sales tax, deposit, refund, and warranty; describe a budget and payment schedule without treating one choice as universal advice; say U.S. dollar amounts intelligibly; and justify a fictional purchasing decision from complete evidence.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered U.S. dollar wording, money and bill countability, and an exact price comparison.">
    <h3>Reactivate numbers, quantifiers, and comparison</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Money foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="one thousand two hundred fifty dollars and fifty cents">one thousand two hundred fifty dollars and fifty cents</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="much money and many bills">much money and many bills</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="costs forty-five dollars more">costs forty-five dollars more</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="one thousand and two hundred and fifty dollars point fifty">one thousand and two hundred and fifty dollars point fifty</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In this U.S. dollar context, read <span class="ex">$1,250.50</span> as <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="one thousand two hundred fifty dollars and fifty cents" data-hint="In common U.S. number wording, do not add and inside the whole-number part; use and between the dollar and cent amounts." data-fix="$1,250.50 is one thousand two hundred fifty dollars and fifty cents.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Complete the quantity contrast: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="much money and many bills" data-hint="Money is uncountable here; bills are separate countable items." data-fix="Use much money and many bills.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A chair is $80 and a desk is $125. The desk <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="costs forty-five dollars more" data-hint="Subtract 80 from 125, then use costs plus the difference plus more." data-fix="The desk costs forty-five dollars more than the chair.">choose</button> than the chair.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice a price, added charges, total cost, and available amount</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Harbor Learning Center quote</h3>
    <p><b>Mara:</b> <span class="ex">“The projector’s listed price is $480. Sales tax is $38.40, and delivery is another $20, so the cash total is $538.40.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Joel:</b> <span class="ex">“The equipment budget has $550 available this month. We can afford the cash total within that limit, but we still need to compare quality and useful life.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Vendor:</b> <span class="ex">“The installment option is twelve payments of $47, or $564 in total. That total includes tax and delivery, and we do not charge a separate setup fee.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which number is the listed price before tax and delivery?</li>
      <li>Which amounts are added to the listed price?</li>
      <li>Which payment route fits the center’s current $550 limit?</li>
      <li>Does fitting the limit prove that the projector is good value?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the listed price, sales tax, cash total, and installment total rather than treating every number as the same kind of cost.">
    <h3>Discovery check: what does each amount represent?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Quote amount choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="listed price before added charges">listed price before added charges</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="sales tax">sales tax</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="cash total after tax and delivery">cash total after tax and delivery</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="total of the twelve installments">total of the twelve installments</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The $480 amount is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="listed price before added charges" data-hint="This number appears before sales tax and delivery are added." data-fix="$480 is the listed price before added charges.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The $38.40 amount is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="sales tax" data-hint="The quote names this location-based charge directly." data-fix="$38.40 is the sales tax in the fictional quote.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The $538.40 amount is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="cash total after tax and delivery" data-hint="Add $480, $38.40, and $20." data-fix="$538.40 is the cash total after tax and delivery.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The $564 amount is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="total of the twelve installments" data-hint="Multiply twelve payments by $47." data-fix="$564 is the total of the twelve $47 installments.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Map money coming in and going out</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>core meaning</th><th>useful boundary</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">salary</span></td><td>regular pay for a job, often expressed as a yearly or monthly amount</td><td>not the total of every kind of money a person receives</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">wage</span></td><td>pay often calculated by the hour, day, or unit of work</td><td><span class="ex">wages</span> can name the total pay received</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">income</span></td><td>money received from work or other stated sources</td><td>normally uncountable for one person’s total, although <span class="ex">incomes</span> is possible when comparing groups or sources</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">revenue</span></td><td>money a business or organization receives from its activities before expenses are subtracted</td><td>not automatically profit and not the usual word for an employee’s pay</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">expense</span></td><td>a cost paid or owed for a purpose</td><td>one expense is countable; several expenses are outgoings</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Profit</span> is what remains for a business after relevant expenses are subtracted from revenue. A person can also describe money <span class="ex">coming in</span> and <span class="ex">going out</span>. These phrases explain direction without requiring a technical accounting discussion.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished fixed job pay, hourly pay, total personal income, and outgoing expenses from the supplied payment structure.">
    <h3>Choose the inflow or outflow the record establishes</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Income and expense choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="salary">salary</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hourly wage">hourly wage</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="income">income</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="expenses">expenses</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Alina’s employment contract gives her the same $3,600 before tax each month. That regular job pay is her <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="salary" data-hint="Choose regular employment pay stated as a monthly amount." data-fix="$3,600 per month is Alina’s salary.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Devin is paid $22 for each hour worked. The $22 figure is Devin’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hourly wage" data-hint="The pay is calculated for each hour rather than fixed by month." data-fix="$22 per hour is Devin’s hourly wage.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Rae receives a salary from one job and earnings from weekend design projects. Together, those stated inflows form Rae’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="income" data-hint="Choose the broad word for money received from the two stated sources." data-fix="The salary and project earnings form Rae’s income.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>For the fictional studio, rent, electricity, software, and insurance are outgoing <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="expenses" data-hint="These are separate costs the studio pays or owes." data-fix="Rent, electricity, software, and insurance are studio expenses.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Count the item, not every money word</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Money, cash</span>, and the money meaning of <span class="ex">change</span> are normally uncountable: <span class="ex">some money, much cash, a little change</span>. Their units are countable: <span class="ex">three dollars, two coins, four bills</span>. In U.S. English, <span class="ex">bill</span> can mean paper money or a statement showing an amount due, so context matters.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Cost</span> is not always uncountable. Use singular <span class="ex">the total cost</span> for one combined amount and plural <span class="ex">additional costs</span> for separate expenses. <span class="ex">Savings</span> usually names money kept for later and commonly takes a plural verb: <span class="ex">The savings are protected.</span> A <span class="ex">saving</span> can name an amount avoided: <span class="ex">a saving of $20</span>. In <span class="ex">worth $50</span>, <span class="ex">worth</span> does not become a plural money noun.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched uncountable money, countable bills and costs, plural savings, and one measured saving to their grammatical frames.">
    <h3>Choose the countability pattern required by the sentence</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Money countability choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="not much money but many bills">not much money but many bills</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="two additional costs">two additional costs</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="savings are">savings are</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a saving of twenty dollars">a saving of twenty dollars</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The monthly summary shows <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="not much money but many bills" data-hint="Use much with uncountable money, many with countable bills, and but for the contrast." data-fix="The monthly summary shows not much money but many bills.">choose</button>: only $20 remains, while fourteen separate bills arrived.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Tax and delivery are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="two additional costs" data-hint="The sentence names two separate added expenses, so cost is plural." data-fix="Tax and delivery are two additional costs.">choose</button> beyond the listed price.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The center’s emergency <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="savings are" data-hint="The noun savings commonly has plural agreement." data-fix="The center’s emergency savings are in a separate account.">choose</button> in a separate account.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The coupon reduced the total from $120 to $100, which produced <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a saving of twenty dollars" data-hint="Name one measured amount that the buyer did not have to spend." data-fix="The coupon produced a saving of twenty dollars.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Track the roles in every transaction</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>viewpoint</th><th>pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>item or service</td><td><span class="ex">something costs an amount</span></td><td>The repair costs $260.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>seller or provider</td><td><span class="ex">charge someone an amount for something</span></td><td>The supplier charged us $18 for delivery.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>buyer or payer</td><td><span class="ex">pay someone an amount / pay an amount for something</span></td><td>We paid the supplier $400. We paid $400 for the projector.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>use of money</td><td><span class="ex">spend an amount on something</span></td><td>We spent $35 on cables.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>temporary receiver</td><td><span class="ex">borrow something from someone</span></td><td>The center borrowed $200 from the partner fund.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>temporary giver</td><td><span class="ex">lend something to someone / lend someone something</span></td><td>The fund lent $200 to the center. The fund lent the center $200.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>unpaid obligation</td><td><span class="ex">owe someone an amount / owe an amount to someone</span></td><td>The center owes the fund $200.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Pay back</span> means return borrowed money: <span class="ex">The center paid the fund back on Friday</span> or <span class="ex">paid back the $200 on Friday</span>. Borrowing and owing are related in this scenario, but <span class="ex">owe</span> can also describe another unpaid obligation. The context must establish what is due and to whom.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You followed the temporary receiver, temporary giver, unpaid amount, provider’s charge, and buyer’s payment from the stated transaction roles.">
    <h3>Choose the verb pattern from the named role</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Transaction direction choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="borrowed two hundred dollars from">borrowed two hundred dollars from</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="lent two hundred dollars to">lent two hundred dollars to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="owes the fund two hundred dollars">owes the fund two hundred dollars</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="charged the center eighteen dollars for delivery">charged the center eighteen dollars for delivery</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="paid four hundred dollars for the projector">paid four hundred dollars for the projector</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The center temporarily received $200 from the partner fund. The center <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="borrowed two hundred dollars from" data-hint="The center is the temporary receiver, so use borrow plus from." data-fix="The center borrowed two hundred dollars from the partner fund.">choose</button> the partner fund.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fund temporarily gave the center $200. The fund <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="lent two hundred dollars to" data-hint="The fund is the temporary giver, so use lend plus to." data-fix="The fund lent two hundred dollars to the center.">choose</button> the center.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Before repayment, the center <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="owes the fund two hundred dollars" data-hint="Name the person or organization that should receive the unpaid amount." data-fix="Before repayment, the center owes the fund two hundred dollars.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The supplier requested an added $18 because it transported the projector. The supplier <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="charged the center eighteen dollars for delivery" data-hint="Use charge plus customer plus amount plus for the service." data-fix="The supplier charged the center eighteen dollars for delivery.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The center gave the seller $400 in exchange for the projector. It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="paid four hundred dollars for the projector" data-hint="Use pay plus amount plus for the item." data-fix="The center paid four hundred dollars for the projector.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Separate price, total cost, affordability, and value</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A <span class="ex">price</span> is an amount asked for an item or service. The <span class="ex">total cost</span> combines every amount included in the stated comparison, such as tax, required fees, delivery, or interest. <span class="ex">Can afford</span> means the payment fits the resources and priorities in the context. It does not simply mean that enough cash exists at one moment. <span class="ex">Good value</span> means the benefit, quality, useful life, or suitability compares favorably with the cost and alternatives.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Cheap</span> means low in price and can sometimes suggest low quality, depending on tone and context. <span class="ex">Inexpensive</span> more neutrally means not expensive. Neither word proves that an item is good value. <span class="ex">Expensive</span> is relative to a comparison, budget, or expectation; it does not by itself mean wasteful.</p>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You kept the seller’s price, complete total, contextual affordability, and evidence-based value judgment as four different claims.">
    <h3>Choose the claim the evidence supports</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The listed price is $480 before tax and delivery." data-hint="Choose the amount the seller names before the added charges." data-fix="The listed price is $480 before tax and delivery."><p class="quiz-question">1. The tag says $480; tax and delivery are separate. Which statement is precise?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The listed price is $480 before tax and delivery.">The listed price is $480 before tax and delivery.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The complete cash total is $480 after every charge.">The complete cash total is $480 after every charge.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The item is automatically good value because it has a price.">The item is automatically good value because it has a price.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The cash total is $538.40 after the stated tax and delivery charge." data-hint="Combine the listed price with both required added amounts." data-fix="The cash total is $538.40 after the stated tax and delivery charge."><p class="quiz-question">2. What does $538.40 represent in the Harbor quote?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The cash total is $538.40 after the stated tax and delivery charge.">The cash total is $538.40 after the stated tax and delivery charge.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="It is only the $20 delivery fee.">It is only the $20 delivery fee.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="It is the center’s yearly revenue.">It is the center’s yearly revenue.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The center can afford the cash total within the stated $550 equipment limit." data-hint="Compare the $538.40 cash total with the exact $550 limit." data-fix="The center can afford the cash total within the stated $550 equipment limit."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which affordability claim follows from the fictional budget?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The center can afford the cash total within the stated $550 equipment limit.">The center can afford the cash total within the stated $550 equipment limit.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every buyer in every situation can afford the projector.">Every buyer in every situation can afford the projector.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The projector has no cost because the budget exists.">The projector has no cost because the budget exists.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Price alone is insufficient; the center must compare suitability, quality, useful life, and alternatives." data-hint="A value judgment needs evidence about what the buyer receives for the cost." data-fix="Price alone is insufficient; the center must compare suitability, quality, useful life, and alternatives."><p class="quiz-question">4. Does fitting the budget prove good value?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Price alone is insufficient; the center must compare suitability, quality, useful life, and alternatives.">Price alone is insufficient; the center must compare suitability, quality, useful life, and alternatives.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Yes. Anything affordable is automatically the best possible value.">Yes. Anything affordable is automatically the best possible value.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="No. An affordable item can never provide good value.">No. An affordable item can never provide good value.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use U.S. checkout and payment language precisely</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>meaning in this lesson</th><th>do not confuse it with</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">check</span></td><td>the restaurant statement showing what is due in common U.S. English</td><td>a bank check or the act of checking</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">bill</span></td><td>a statement showing an amount due, such as an electric bill; also understood for a restaurant amount</td><td>a receipt that proves payment</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">receipt</span></td><td>a paper or digital record that payment or a transaction occurred</td><td>the unpaid bill or check</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">fee</span></td><td>an amount charged for a service or specific purpose</td><td>the complete price of every item</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">sales tax</span></td><td>tax added to eligible purchases under the stated location’s rules</td><td>a voluntary tip</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">deposit</span></td><td>money paid first to reserve, secure, or begin an agreement, depending on the stated terms</td><td>proof that every later amount has been paid</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">refund</span></td><td>money returned after a payment under the stated policy or agreement</td><td>a discount applied before payment</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">warranty</span></td><td>a written promise covering stated repair or replacement conditions for a period</td><td>a promise that the item can never fail</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A <span class="ex">service charge</span> is an amount added for a service. A <span class="ex">tip</span> is separate unless the exact bill, policy, or local rule says otherwise. Taxes, refund rights, deposits, warranties, and service-charge practices vary by place and agreement, so read the stated terms rather than guessing from the noun.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified the restaurant amount due, proof of payment, returned money, initial reservation payment, and added service amount from their transaction jobs.">
    <h3>Choose the checkout word from the supplied event</h3>
