  <h2>Read a purchasing decision from stated priorities</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Lakeside Learning Center equipment record</h3>
    <p><b>Purpose and limit:</b> The center needs a working projector for classes beginning next month. A grant permits up to $550 for equipment this month. A separate building-repair reserve cannot be used for equipment under the grant rules. The team will not borrow money for this purchase.</p>
    <p><b>Option A, repair:</b> Repair the current five-year-old projector for a total of $260. The repair has a 90-day warranty. The technician estimates another 8 to 12 months of use but does not guarantee that estimate.</p>
    <p><b>Option B, refurbished:</b> Buy an inspected refurbished projector for a $400 listed price, $32 sales tax, and $18 delivery fee. The total is $450, payment is due now, and the product has a one-year warranty.</p>
    <p><b>Option C, new:</b> Buy a new projector for a $648 cash total or twelve installments of $56. The installment total is $672, and the product has a two-year warranty. Both payment routes exceed the current $550 grant limit.</p>
    <p><b>Recorded decision:</b> The team chose Option B. Its reasons were the $450 total, the one-year warranty, the team’s preference not to borrow, and the fact that the amount stays within the equipment grant. The record does not claim that Option B is the best choice for every buyer.</p>
  </div>
