  <h2>Practice money language without disclosing private finances</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to reveal real income, salary, wages, rent, debt, bank balance, savings, credit score, loan, tax record, spending history, family support, financial stress, purchase, employer, account number, or payment problem. This lesson teaches English, not financial, tax, legal, investment, or credit advice. If a prompt feels private, use Harbor Learning Center, Lakeside Learning Center, or another fictional organization. No task requires choosing a financial product or defending a real decision.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Amount map:</b> Label every number as listed price, tax, fee, deposit, installment, refund, payment, or total. Do not compare unlike amounts.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Direction map:</b> Name the giver, receiver, item or service, temporary transfer, unpaid amount, and repayment date before choosing the verb.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Decision map:</b> State the fictional budget, timeframe, required features, risks, payment timing, total cost, and evidence for value.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Advice boundary:</b> Report what fits the supplied priorities. Do not turn one fictional decision into a recommendation for every person.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: deliver a 90-second fictional purchasing briefing</h3>
    <p>Use only the Lakeside record. State the purpose and $550 limit; separate every listed price, tax, fee, cash total, and installment total; identify which options fit the current limit; compare payment timing, warranty, expected useful life, and the team’s no-borrowing preference; report the selected option and its exact reasons; and finish with one limitation on the conclusion. Use at least eight target chunks, including <span class="ex">costs, charged, paid for, can afford, good value, total cost, installment</span>, and <span class="ex">warranty</span>. Make every amount and transaction role audible.</p>
    <p><b>Evidence check:</b> Can the listener recover what the center needs, which money is available for that purpose, what every option costs in total, which extra charges are included, when payment is due, what coverage is stated, which preference affects the decision, and why the choice is specific to this fictional team?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished listed price from total cost, tracked cost-charge-pay roles, preserved borrow-lend-owe direction, separated affordability from value, used cost countability accurately, and interpreted installments and interest without giving advice.">
    <h3>Final check: choose the complete financial account</h3>
